Z||000000||88 Fiero GT Turbo||08-02-2000||07:15 PM||||In your opinion what is the best engine setup for a 2.8 v6 with a Miller Woods turbo.  I am looking to gain as much horse power as I can but still want to be able to drive it on the streets.<P>P.S.  by setup I mean crank, cam, pistons, pushrods, and so on.  You get the idea the internals of the motor.<P>Thanks for all your help<P>Later, <BR>Jack||||reg||6
Z||000001||hugh||08-02-2000||08:08 PM||||If you want reliability,don't scrimp on the internal parts.If your crank needs to be cut ,have the rod throws radiused for strength.Stay at about8.5 to 9.0 to 1 compression ratio max.The cam is the least important part as far as power output is concerned,but a roller cam would be a good idea.Just watch your overlap,too much will kill any boost you might try to get.An intercooler will help prevent detonation and give a big increase in horsepower because you can run more boost.The most important thing to get the air into the turbo and then into the engine is to prevent turbulence.Smooth out any entrance into your air stream.You'll be surprised with little or no turbo lag.On the exhaust side,go with as big an exhaust as you can.Stainless would be the best.My grand National is on it's 4th exhaust system(stainless)Finally,get yourself the best control system you can afford,it will be money well spent.   ||||reg||1
